{"prdMarkdown":"# PRD: User Authentication & Scoped Sessions\n\n## Problem\nCurrently in Spec Clarity, clarification sessions are unauthenticated. Any user who knows or guesses a session ID can view, mutate, or trigger write-back operations on that session via `src/routes/sessions.js`. As users generate proprietary feature specifications, architectural assessments, and repository-level insights, there is no access control protecting these resources, creating a data exposure and integrity risk.\n\n## Goals\n- Scope all spec-clarification sessions to authenticated user accounts via email/password authentication.\n- Prevent unauthorized users or anonymous visitors from accessing, modifying, or viewing another user's sessions.\n- Protect all session retrieval, mutation, and GitHub write-back endpoints so they are accessible only by the verified session owner.\n- Provide an authentication user interface within `public/index.html` allowing users to register, log in, log out, and retain session state across page reloads.\n\n## Non-goals\n- OAuth/SSO integration (e.g., GitHub, Google login) for v1 (email and password authentication is sufficient for the initial implementation).\n- Organization/team multi-tenancy or role-based access control (RBAC).\n- Granular session-sharing permissions (e.g., \"share read-only link\" or public collaboration links).\n- Self-serve password reset flows or email verification services in v1.\n\n## User Impact\n- **New Users:** Must sign up with an email and password in `public/index.html` before creating or managing clarification sessions.\n- **Returning Users:** Will log in to access their previously created sessions and dashboard.\n- **Security & Privacy:** Feature requests, repository analysis, and open questions will no longer be publicly exposed or modifiable by third parties holding the session ID.\n- **GitHub Write-back:** Calls to post issues to GitHub will be strictly restricted to the user who owns that clarification session, eliminating unauthorized writeback attempts.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- `[PRODUCT_DECISION]` **Handling Legacy Sessions:** How should existing records in `src/db/schema.sql` with a `NULL` `user_id` be handled upon migration? Options include purging them, keeping them read-only for public access, or allowing the first authenticated user to claim them.\n- `[PRODUCT_DECISION]` **Sign-up Access Controls:** Is registration completely open to any public visitor, or should self-registration be gated (e.g., invite-only, domain whitelisting)?\n- `[PRODUCT_DECISION]` **Session Expiration & Timeout UX:** What is the target session inactivity timeout (e.g., 7 days vs. 24 hours), and when a session expires, should users receive an in-app re-authentication modal or be redirected to the login view?","trdMarkdown":"# TRD: User Authentication & Session Scoping\n\n## Architecture Notes\n\n### 1. Database Schema & Migrations\n- Update `src/db/schema.sql` (and migration runner `src/db/migrate.js`) to introduce a `users` table:\n  ```sql\n  CREATE TABLE users (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    password_hash VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT NOW()\n  );\n  ```\n- Add a `user_id INTEGER REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE` column to the `sessions` table.\n- Implement a migration strategy in `src/db/migrate.js` for existing sessions with `NULL` `user_id` values (e.g., associating them with a default admin user, archiving them, or dropping unowned test data).\n\n### 2. Authentication & Route Guard Middleware\n- Add authentication middleware (e.g., `src/middleware/auth.js`) that validates user credentials or tokens on every protected route in `src/routes/sessions.js`.\n- Scope all CRUD operations in `src/routes/sessions.js` by verifying `session.user_id === req.user.id`.\n- Explicitly guard the GitHub writeback endpoint in `src/routes/sessions.js` ensuring that requests to write to GitHub require both valid session ownership and the provided PAT.\n\n### 3. Server Configuration & Package Dependencies\n- Update `package.json` to include dependencies for:\n  - Password hashing: `bcrypt` or `argon2`.\n  - Session token management: `jsonwebtoken` (or cookie session libraries).\n- Update `env.example` to declare auth-related environment variables, including `JWT_SECRET` (or `SESSION_SECRET`) and `TOKEN_EXPIRY`.\n\n### 4. Client-Side Authentication Flow\n- Update `public/index.html` to include views/modals for login, registration, and logout.\n- Persist auth tokens (or HTTP-only cookies) and handle `401 Unauthorized` and `403 Forbidden` API responses gracefully by presenting login states or clearing invalid local state.\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n\n- **Missing Dependencies (`package.json`):** `package.json` currently only contains `cors`, `dotenv`, `express`, `ignore`, and `pg`. Password hashing and token management packages must be installed.\n- **Database Schema Migration (`src/db/schema.sql`, `src/db/migrate.js`):** Adding a foreign key constraint `user_id` to existing sessions will fail or leave orphaned `NULL` entries unless migration logic handles existing legacy rows.\n- **Session ID Enumeration (`src/routes/sessions.js`):** Requesting a session ID owned by another user could leak the existence of private sessions if a `403 Forbidden` is returned; returning `404 Not Found` prevents unauthorized ID enumeration.\n- **Write-back Hijacking (`src/routes/sessions.js`, `src/pipeline/githubWriteback.js`):** Prior to this change, anyone with a session ID and any GitHub PAT could trigger writeback. Ownership checks must precede `src/pipeline/githubWriteback.js` execution.\n- **Frontend Error & State Handling (`public/index.html`):** The single-page client currently assumes all session operations succeed anonymously. It needs error boundaries for session expiration and unauthenticated route requests.\n- **Environment Secrets (`env.example`):** Missing configurations for auth secret keys. Failure to define `JWT_SECRET` in deployment must cause startup assertions to fail fast.\n- **Input Sanitization & Normalization:** Lack of email normalization (case insensitivity, whitespace trimming) and password strength rules during registration risks account collisions and weak credential storage.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- `[ENGINEERING_DECISION]` **Token Transport Mechanism:** Should session authentication use stateless HTTP-only cookies containing signed JWTs, standard `Authorization: Bearer <token>` headers stored in client memory/localStorage, or server-side session stores in Postgres?\n- `[ENGINEERING_DECISION]` **Unauthorized vs. Forbidden Responses:** When an authenticated user requests a session belonging to another user in `src/routes/sessions.js`, should the API return `404 Not Found` (to avoid leaking valid session UUIDs/IDs) or `403 Forbidden`?\n\n## Estimate\n- **Expected Duration:** 2-3 days.\n- **Upper Bound (Worst Case):** Up to 7 days (accounting for legacy session data migration, client UI integration in `public/index.html`, and edge-case testing around token expiration and writeback permissions)."}